/-
OnBytes/Model/TestWeightsRun.lean: values of the C++ verifier at the end of the three sub-proofs
with claims on the weights, on an honest proof for made-up weights at a small configuration (the
shape TestWeights.smallKit with 11 column bits). The tests run when this file compiles.

The program does not import this file.

The values were printed by added print statements, which are not part of the repository, in
wsForkEnd (verifier/src/vf_weights_layout.h) and wsPending (vf_weights_pending_claim.h), in a whole
call of another state of cp_verify. The public files were those of
prover/tests/configurations/pinned_no_proof_of_work. Key, entry and proof were made by a build for
tests of that same state of cp_commit (-weights made-up-blocks) and cp_prove, under a test
seed; they and the printed lines are not in the repository. Of the values below the verifier reads
only fvp, which is also pTarget, from the proof. It draws rm, beta and the second element of rho
from the hash chain; psi0 is the last element of rm, and pBeta is beta. It computes the rest of rho
(1 and the powers of the second element; givenH has one claim, so its rho is 1 alone), epsT (from
rm, the layout and the claims' factors) and ka (rho times epsT). The whole numbers slotIn, slotOut,
sFv, ui, claims, first, last, matrixRows, kRow and tensors do not depend on the proof: they follow
from the sub-proofs' plans, from the parameter file's lists of calls and from the layout, none of
which depends on the proof. The claims' factors are not among the values, so the tests here check
only what can be checked without them:
 1. the scalar of each claim is rho times the value of its factors on the columns, by this library's
    product in the field; the coefficients rho are the powers of one element;
 2. the merge takes wsForkSlots slots, and the slot of fv' is the last of them;
 3. the stretch of rows that the claims' units span, by this library's layout;
 4. the number of vectors of product form into which the stretch is cut;
 5. the row's point, factor and value are the merge's.
The second half of 2, and 5, compare printed values with each other. They record what the C++
verifier does and run nothing of this library; OnBytes/Model/TestMerge.lean asks the same of this
library.
-/
import OnBytes.Model.Pending
import OnBytes.Model.TestWeightsGiven

namespace OnBytes.Model.TestWeightsRun

/-- The printed values of one sub-proof. -/
structure Given where
  /-- the next slot on entry to wsForkEnd -/
  slotIn : Nat
  /-- the next slot on return -/
  slotOut : Nat
  /-- the slot of fv' -/
  sFv : Nat
  /-- for each claim: its unit -/
  ui : List Nat
  /-- for each claim: its coefficient -/
  rho : List E
  /-- for each claim: the value of its factors on the columns at the merge's point -/
  epsT : List E
  /-- for each claim: the scalar of what is left of it -/
  ka : List E
  /-- the merge's point -/
  rm : Array E
  /-- fv', as it was shown -/
  fvp : E
  /-- the factor of the mask of fv' -/
  beta : E
  /-- wsPending: the number of claims -/
  claims : Nat
  /-- the first row of the claims' units -/
  first : Nat
  /-- the row after their last -/
  last : Nat
  /-- the number of rows of the matrix -/
  matrixRows : Nat
  /-- the number of bits of a row's number -/
  kRow : Nat
  /-- the number of vectors of product form -/
  tensors : Nat
  /-- the first coordinate of the pending row's point -/
  psi0 : E
  /-- the pending row's factor of the mask entry -/
  pBeta : E
  /-- the pending row's right side -/
  pTarget : E

